{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789342987,"nonce":"kverqtexSx35pWzpbx6bLUPzB04=","title":"fib(40) with pure-call PRE off: 340 ms on Cranelift, wanted 200","message":"The `fib_no_pure_call_pre` row is the call-and-inline test: with PRE off\nnothing collapses the recursion, so the number is what the inliner and\nthe backend make of 331M calls. ZynML sat at 340 ms, Python at 449 (its\npending-exception check after every call; 68ba324).\n\nHand-written C on this machine, clang -O2 -fno-inline, for the shapes the\ninliner can produce: depth-1 self-inlining 300 ms, depth-2 170 ms, the\naccumulator-loop form 280 ms. Apple clang's own -O3 on plain fib is 610\nms here (no recursive inlining), so 200 ms means depth 2 with codegen\nclose to clang's.\n\nDone here: a second round of recursive self-inlining (the round's own\nsize limits bound it: a depth-1 body over 96 instructions or with more\nthan 4 sites stays at depth 1), and empty blocks threaded away in\ncfg_simplify (the base-case paths landed on chains of two or three\njumps). Result: ZynML 340 to 249 ms; Python, with the pending checks\ngone, 449 to 249 ms. Depth 3 measured 226 ms at 13x the compile time\nand 64 call sites; not taken.\n\nWhat is left between 249 and the C depth-2 shape at 170, read off the\nvcode (`ZYNTAX_DUMP_VCODE=fib`): one `mov` per virtual node\nrematerialising `n` into the return register (regalloc2 honouring the\n`return n` constraint at every base case), and 6 callee-saved registers\nsaved and restored per frame for the partial sums live across the 16\ncalls. Both are Cranelift's; the HIR has no more to give at this depth.\nThe LLVM tier does its own call CSE and reports 0.27 ms for this row,\nwhich measures nothing about calls.","files":null}]}